Modern kernel installation
==========================

The modern compatibility path targets unpatched 64-bit Linux 6.18 and later. See docu/modern-kernel.rst
for the support matrix, durability/discard behavior, secure network setup,
release checks and remaining limitations. That document supersedes the
legacy manual's pre-patch, SIO and VM recommendations for the modern path.

Install configured headers for the exact target kernel, make, Perl, and the
compiler family used for that kernel. Module.symvers is required; merely
running modules_prepare is insufficient when symbol versions are enabled.
For a Clang kernel, for example:

    make -C kernel KDIR=/lib/modules/7.2.9-2-cachyos/build LLVM=1 W=1 -j8

For a GCC kernel, omit LLVM=1. The result is kernel/mars.ko. Use a fresh staged
source directory per target kernel (or clean before changing KDIR):

    python3 scripts/stage-source.py /tmp/mars-build --version local-review
    make -C /tmp/mars-build/kernel KDIR=/lib/modules/$(uname -r)/build W=1

External builds validate kernel dependencies and MARS option values against
kernel/Kconfig. Override options through the environment, for example:

    MARS_DEBUG=y MARS_DEBUG_MEM=y make -C kernel KDIR=... LLVM=1 W=1

The configuration header is regenerated when overrides change. Explicit
options whose dependencies are disabled fail the build. An external build
cannot enable missing kernel facilities: enable them in the target kernel.
MARS_DEBUG_DEVEL_VIA_SAY is a historic file logger; ordinary diagnostics use
the kernel log. Optional legacy paths require their own runtime validation.

Module installation and signing
-------------------------------

Build Debian packages with dpkg-buildpackage -b -us -uc; see debian/README.md.
For a local DKMS installation, choose a unique version and stage the sources:

    python3 scripts/stage-source.py /tmp/mars-dkms-source --version 0.1-local1
    sudo cp -a /tmp/mars-dkms-source /usr/src/mars-0.1-local1
    sudo dkms add -m mars -v 0.1-local1
    sudo dkms build -m mars -v 0.1-local1 -k "$(uname -r)"
    sudo dkms install -m mars -v 0.1-local1 -k "$(uname -r)"

DKMS uses the target kernel's compiler family; LLVM kernels also require
clang, llvm and lld. Configure DKMS's mok_signing_key and mok_certificate in
/etc/dkms/framework.conf according to your distribution's DKMS package.
Enroll the corresponding public certificate using the distribution's Secure
Boot enrollment procedure, then rebuild/reinstall. Never package private
signing keys. Check the installed artifact before loading:

    modinfo -F vermagic mars
    modinfo -F signer mars
    mokutil --sb-state

A nonempty signer only establishes that a signature exists; the booted
kernel must trust that certificate. Validate load, rebuild after a kernel
update, and removal in a disposable VM with signature enforcement enabled.
Manual signing, when needed, uses the target kernel's scripts/sign-file:

    /path/to/kernel/scripts/sign-file sha256 private-key.pem certificate.der kernel/mars.ko

Signing references: https://github.com/dkms-project/dkms#module-signing and
https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/module-signing.html.

Sign before compressing/installing the module. Reproducible unsigned build
metadata does not make independently signed module bytes identical.

Cluster setup
-------------

Use dedicated backing devices of equal size and a separate ext4 filesystem
mounted at /mars with enough room for transaction logs and outages. The
default configured emergency thresholds total 20 GiB; plan capacity above
that minimum. Backing devices must not be mounted directly while MARS owns
them. Never place /mars on a MARS resource.

Install userspace/marsadm as /usr/sbin/marsadm. Configure WireGuard and the
module's network policy before creating or joining the cluster, as described
in docu/modern-kernel.rst. Node names must resolve to
cluster addresses consistently. The following commands create metadata and
write to the specified backing storage; use new, dedicated devices.

On the primary:

    marsadm create-cluster
    modprobe mars

On each secondary, with /mars mounted and the secure network ready:

    marsadm join-cluster PRIMARY_HOSTNAME

Native cluster join loads the module if necessary and contacts the primary.
The historic SSH/rsync fallback has been removed. Module options from
/etc/modprobe.d/mars.conf must therefore be ready before join-cluster runs.

On the primary:

    marsadm create-resource myspace /dev/vg-x/myspace

On each secondary:

    marsadm join-resource myspace /dev/vg-x/myspace

Wait for initial synchronization before relying on the secondary. Application
I/O goes through /dev/mars/myspace on the primary. Monitor transaction log
capacity, replica lag, kernel errors and emergency mode. Use mars-status --json for
the versioned monitoring schema. marsadm cron and
marsadm log-delete-all all maintain logs; the Debian tools package includes
cron entries. Do not delete logfiles outside the documented recovery tools.

The packaged mars.service is disabled by default. After configuring storage,
networking, cluster metadata and application shutdown ordering, enable it
explicitly. It requires /mars to be a mountpoint and /mars/uuid to exist as
a symlink, loads MARS on start, and runs marsadm down all before unloading.
Services mounting/exporting resources must specify After=mars.service and
Requires=mars.service so they stop first. The service never creates a cluster
or automatically promotes resources. Failed demotion must be investigated;
do not force module removal or unplug backing storage.

Validation
----------

Run python3 -m unittest discover -s test_suite/build -v for build-tool checks.
See test_suite/qemu/README for disposable VM tests. Module loads and storage
tests belong in disposable guests, including DKMS/signing checks; the build
scripts do not load a module or modify host block devices.

Legacy kernels
--------------

Historic kernel support, pre-patches and the original administration manual
remain in pre-patches/ and docu/. They are not a current compatibility
promise. The old syscall-based kernel AIO backend is disabled on modern
kernels; regular files and transaction logs use SIO with kernel_read,
kernel_write and fsync, and backing block devices use BIO. Measure workload
latency/throughput before adopting an untested deployment configuration.
